A passive wheel listener no longer makes the page wait for the main thread to
scroll.

Without event regions (that is, everywhere but Apple's platforms) the
scrolling tree learns where wheel listeners are from
ScrollingCoordinator::absoluteEventTrackingRegionsForFrame(), and that put
the box of every node with a wheel or mousewheel listener into the
synchronous region, passive or not.  A wheel event there is not scrolled by
the scrolling thread: it is sent to the main thread, scrolled there after
dispatch, and the next one waits for the reply.  A listener on the window or
the document covers the whole page.  microsoft.com has three, all passive --
one on the window from its analytics plugin, one on the document from the
Clarity session recorder, one on the site header -- so every wheel step of
that page waited for whatever the main thread was doing.  Measured with this
engine's upstream build on a desktop host (threaded scrolling on): all wheel
events of the page went through WebPage::wheelEvent on the main thread, none
through the scrolling tree, and a 1.5 s script on the main thread stopped
the page for 1.5 s; with the passive listeners not registered, the events
went to the scrolling thread and the page kept scrolling through the same
script.  On a slower machine the page's own scroll and timer work is that
script.

A passive listener cannot cancel the scroll, so only nodes with a listener
that can stay in the synchronous region; the document's bounds go into the
asynchronous region when it has wheel listeners at all.  An event there is
scrolled by the scrolling thread and dispatched on the main thread without
being waited for (WheelEventProcessingSteps::NonBlockingDOMEventDispatch,
the path event regions already take for passive listeners), so passive
listeners still get every event, as non-cancelable events, as the
specification has it.

